<ArticleLayout title="MongoDB Locking and Concurrency: Diagnose the Contended Operation" description="An updated guide to MongoDB WiredTiger concurrency, write conflicts, lock waits and the evidence needed to investigate throughput drops." eyebrow="Database archive · MongoDB" published="2025-07-19" updated="2026-10-13" legacy ctaTitle="Need an independent database performance review?" ctaDescription="Describe the workload and the symptom. The first step is confirming whether a bounded investigation fits the platform and evidence available.">
  <h2>Do not treat every slow write as a collection lock</h2>
  <p>MongoDB's WiredTiger engine uses document-level concurrency control for writes and intent locks at broader levels. Many operations on different documents can run concurrently. A slow or retried write may come from a hot document, a long transaction, resource pressure or an application retry pattern; a simplistic lock-mode table will not identify which one.</p>
  <p>Write conflicts and lock waits are different signals. WiredTiger can detect a conflict between operations and retry one; application-level transactions may need explicit retry handling. An operation waiting for a lock should be investigated alongside its command, duration and competing activity.</p>

  <h2>Separate lock waits from write conflicts</h2>
  <p>A lock wait means an operation is waiting for a lock mode to become available. A write conflict means concurrent work touched the same document or snapshot and one operation must retry. Neither signal alone proves that a collection is globally serialized. Correlate the event with the affected command, document access pattern, transaction duration and CPU or storage pressure.</p>

  <h2>Evidence to collect during the event</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the slow endpoint or job, affected collection, time window and whether latency is in reads, writes or transaction commits.</li>
    <li>Inspect current operations and lock statistics, using the deployment's supported tooling and permissions. Compare with CPU, cache pressure, disk activity and transaction metrics.</li>
    <li>Look for repeated updates to the same document, long-running transactions, large batch operations and retries that multiply traffic.</li>
    <li>Change one cause at a time, then compare throughput, error/retry rate and tail latency over similar traffic.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>On a deployment that permits it, the <a href="https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/currentop/"><code>$currentOp</code> aggregation stage</a> can show active operations, <code>waitingForLock</code> and per-operation lock statistics. Scope the query to the incident window and follow your deployment's privilege and observability rules. MongoDB recommends this stage over the older <code>currentOp</code> command.</p>
  <pre><code>{`db.getSiblingDB('admin').aggregate([
  &#123; $currentOp: &#123; allUsers: true, idleConnections: false &#125; &#125;,
  &#123; $match: &#123; waitingForLock: true &#125; &#125;,
  &#123; $project: &#123; opid: 1, op: 1, ns: 1, secs_running: 1,
                waitingForLock: 1, lockStats: 1 &#125; &#125;
]);`}</code></pre>
  <p>For a hot-document pattern, first reduce unnecessary updates or shorten the transaction that owns the document. For a broad throughput drop, compare cache, disk and application retry rate before redesigning locking. A version field can protect an application-level update from overwriting another change, but it does not replace a diagnosis of server contention.</p>
  <p>MongoDB's <a href="https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/wiredtiger/">WiredTiger documentation</a> describes document-level concurrency and transparent retries. Its <a href="https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/faq/concurrency/">concurrency FAQ</a> explains lock visibility and monitoring options.</p>
  <p>This article is retained for readers of the original DatabaseDeveloper publication. The current consulting practice is <a href="/postgresql-performance-investigation">PostgreSQL-first</a>; other database work is scoped individually.</p>
